! The PAW twin of `pt-soc-nosym.in`, from `test-suite/pw_spinorbit/spinorbit-paw.in`.
! Same cell and same settings; what changes is that the overlap operator carries
! `qq_so` from a PAW dataset rather than an ultrasoft one, and the projection is
! `<phi|S|psi>` -- so `S` being right is part of what is being compared.
!
! `nosym`/`noinv` and an unshifted grid, because a spinor projection cannot yet
! be symmetrised here: `sym_proj_so` averages over the point group with the SU(2)
! representation of each operation beside the rotation of the harmonics, and
! nothing in defumat builds those. Run this way both codes use the whole grid and
! neither averages, so the comparison is of the projection itself. `projwfc.x`
! is given `lsym = .false.` to match.
!
! 2x2x2 rather than 4x4x4: what is being compared is a projection at fixed
! states, so the k-set only has to be the same one on both sides.
!
! Platinum's fully-relativistic dataset carries 5D(j=3/2), 5D(j=5/2) and 6S with
! non-negative occupations, so the projection has **12** columns -- 4 + 6 + 2 --
! and not twice the scalar 11. That distinction is the case's own reason for
! being: a dataset whose two j channels are separate PP_CHI entries is where
! `n_atom_wfc`'s noncollinear count stops being a doubling.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 7.42, nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    lspinorb = .true.,
    noncolin = .true.,
    starting_magnetization = 0.0,
    occupations = 'smearing',
    degauss = 0.02,
    smearing = 'mp',
    ecutwfc = 30.0,
    ecutrho = 250.0,
    nosym = .true.,
    noinv = .true.,
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0e-8
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
Pt  195.08   Pt.rel-pbe-n-kjpaw_psl.0.1.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS {alat}
Pt  0.0000000   0.00000000   0.0
K_POINTS AUTOMATIC
2 2 2 0 0 0
